            <ArticleTitle>The Role of Attachment in Body Weight and Weight Loss in Bariatric Patients.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1007/s11695-017-2796-1</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="PURPOSE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">The aim of this study is to explore the role of attachment styles in obesity.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MATERIAL AND METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">The present study explored differences in insecure attachment styles between an obese sample waiting for bariatric surgery (n = 195) and an age, sex and height matched normal weight control group (n = 195). It then explored the role of attachment styles in predicting change in BMI 1 year post bariatric surgery (n = 143).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The bariatric group reported significantly higher levels of anxious attachment and lower levels of avoidant attachment than the control non-obese group. Baseline attachment styles did not, however, predict change in BMI post surgery.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Attachment style is different in those that are already obese from those who are not. Attachment was not related to weight loss post surgery.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
